(vector (map (:adr/id 2799008341), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-8341 — Mobile Farm and Forestry Plant Operators Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/status :accepted), (:adr/date "2026-07-17"), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8341)), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000 2607152500)), (:adr/related (vector "adr-2799005411-cloud-itonami-isco-5411-firefighters-coverage" "adr-2799005413-cloud-itonami-isco-5413-prison-guards-coverage")), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-8341), (:actor/name "Mobile Farm and Forestry Plant Operations Scheduling and Logistics Coordination Practice"), (:actor/domain :agriculture/mobile-plant-operations-coordination), (:actor/isco-08 "8341"), (:actor/wave 2), (:actor/robotics? true), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-service-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-maintenance-order)), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :no-operator :no-actuation :op-not-allowlisted :unknown-equipment :equipment-wrong-operator :scope-violation)), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "finalize the equipment movement" "finalize the equipment-operation decision" "finalize equipment movement" "initiate the equipment movement" "directly operate the equipment" "directly dispatch the equipment" "override the operator's on-site safety judgment" "override the operator's safety judgment" "override the operator's on-site judgment")), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-maintenance-order 2000}"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8341"), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-8341"), (:test/counts "{:total 19, :assertions 44, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :closed-op-allowlist :maintenance-cost-threshold-escalation :equipment-provenance :rogue-advisor-end-to-end)), (:test/simulator :farmforestryops.actor-test), (:db/id -1), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799008341: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8341 Mobile Farm and Forestry Plant Operators scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8341\n-- Mobile Farm and Forestry Plant Operators** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8341\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"8341\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Mobile Farm and Forestry Plant\nOperators\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR\nthe entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies\n[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`.\nThis ADR and its companion registry edit promote the `8341` entry to\n`:implemented`. Under `kotoba.occupation.wave` (ADR-2607121000 section\n3), sub-major `83` (drivers and mobile plant operators) falls in ISCO\nWave 2 (coordination-logistics), consistent with this actor's\ncoordination-only scope.\n\nThis is a retry: a prior session in this same fleet batch created the\nGitHub repository `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8341` and pushed a\ncomplete implementation to its `main` branch (commit `665daa8`, \"feat:\nscaffold cloud-itonami-isco-8341 (Mobile Farm and Forestry Plant\nOperators)\") before being cut off by a session rate limit mid-task,\nleaving the registry promotion and this ADR unfinished. This session\nre-verified the pushed repository in full against a fresh clone (all\nfour `.cljc` modules, both test files, the full doc set) rather than\ntrusting the prior session's own report, and confirmed it independently\ngreen (`clojure -M:test`: 19 tests, 44 assertions, 0 failures, 0\nerrors) before proceeding to the registry promotion and this ADR.\n\nMobile Farm and Forestry Plant Operators operate heavy, mobile\nequipment -- tractors, combine harvesters, forestry skidders and\nfeller-bunchers -- with real physical-safety stakes: rollover on\nuneven terrain, entanglement in power take-off shafts and moving\nmechanisms, and collision risk around other crew and bystanders in an\nactive field or forest work site. This actor is deliberately\n**scheduling/logistics coordination ONLY, never equipment-operation\nauthority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate\nexercising, or propose exercising a decision that directly finalizes\nan equipment-operation/movement decision (e.g. deciding a tractor\nshould start a pass, a harvester should engage its header, a skidder\nshould move a load) or that overrides an operator's own on-site safety\njudgment (e.g. instructing an operator to proceed despite the\noperator's own assessment of unsafe terrain, weather or equipment\ncondition). These two categories are not merely gated behind\nescalation -- no op anywhere in the closed proposal-op allowlist\nresembles either of them, and any proposal that reaches for either\ncategory through free-text rationale is an unconditional, permanent,\nnon-overridable hard block, independent of the op-allowlist check.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and for the\nclosed-allowlist + registered-AND-verified provenance + content-based\nscope-exclusion SAFETY PATTERN is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals) -- cloned and read in full this\nsession (`store.cljc`/`advisor.cljc`/`governor.cljc`/`actor.cljc`/both\ntest files/full doc set: blueprint.edn, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\nCONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, LICENSE, README.md, SECURITY.md,\ndocs/business-model.md, docs/operator-guide.md). The `store`/`advisor`/\n`governor`/`actor` module split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph shape,\nand full documentation set trace back to that same reference.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8341` as an OSS\nscheduling/logistics coordination actor for mobile farm and forestry\nplant operators, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as\nevery prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Farm/Forestry\nOps Advisor ⊣ FarmForestryOpsGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to the reference's `deps.edn` --\n`5b4e5e70837b849b14b6a5800894e3be202ad5fa` -- a different library\ncoordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*`\nactor family), with an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop\napproval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-service-record` -- equipment usage/incident-report data\n  logging (usage hours, condition, incident metadata -- never a\n  movement command)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- operator-roster/field-work scheduling\n  proposal (scheduling logistics only, never a live movement\n  authorization)\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface an equipment-defect, terrain-hazard\n  or operator-fatigue concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-maintenance-order` -- equipment maintenance procurement\n  proposal\n\nNo op on this allowlist finalizes an equipment-operation/movement\ndecision or overrides an operator's on-site safety judgment -- these\ntwo categories are structurally absent from the allowlist entirely, not\nmerely gated behind escalation. The reference mock advisor's `infer`\nfunction only ever emits one of these four ops, driven by a `cond->`\nexpression building a fixed proposal shape with no branch producing any\nother op. If any code path (a forged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a\nmisbehaving fork) ever produced an out-of-allowlist op, the governor's\nclosed-allowlist HARD check (`:op-not-allowlisted`) rejects it\nunconditionally and permanently\n(`farmforestryops.governor-test/hard-on-op-not-allowlisted`).\n\n### Five HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **No operator** -- any proposal must resolve to an independently\n   registered operator record (`store/operator`). Never trusts the\n   proposal's own claim of who the operator is\n   (`farmforestryops.governor-test/hard-on-unregistered-operator`).\n   This directly implements the task's \"operator/equipment record must\n   be independently verified/registered before any action\" invariant.\n2. **No actuation** -- any `:effect` other than `:propose` is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance -- the\n   governor never dispatches hardware and never itself operates\n   equipment) (`.../hard-on-no-actuation-violation`).\n3. **Op not allowlisted** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op\n   allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently rejects any\n   attempt to slip in an equipment-operation/movement-finalizing op\n   under a new name (e.g. `:finalize-equipment-movement`, exercised\n   directly by `.../hard-on-op-not-allowlisted`).\n4. **Equipment basis** -- a proposal must cite a REGISTERED piece of\n   equipment currently assigned to this request's operator\n   (`:unknown-equipment` for an unregistered equipment id,\n   `:equipment-wrong-operator` for equipment assigned to a different\n   operator than the one making the request)\n   (`.../hard-on-unknown-equipment`, `.../hard-on-foreign-equipment`).\n5. **Scope exclusion** -- independent of the op-allowlist check, any\n   proposal whose free-text `:rationale` names a finalization/execution\n   action for an equipment-operation/movement decision or an override\n   of an operator's on-site safety judgment (e.g. \"initiate the\n   equipment movement\", \"finalize the equipment-operation decision\",\n   \"override the operator's on-site safety judgment\") is a HARD,\n   PERMANENT block, evaluated unconditionally on content\n   (`.../hard-on-scope-violation-finalizing-equipment-movement`,\n   `.../hard-on-scope-violation-overriding-operator-safety-judgment`).\n   This is defense-in-depth on top of check 3: even if a future change\n   somehow widened the allowlist, content-based rejection still\n   independently blocks equipment-movement-finalization or\n   safety-override language wherever it appears in free text.\n\n### Two always-escalate/threshold gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally)\n  (`.../always-escalates-flag-safety-concern-even-at-high-confidence`).\n  This is the only channel by which the robot may surface an\n  equipment-defect, terrain-hazard or operator-fatigue observation --\n  it never resolves the concern itself, and the actor's role ends at\n  \"here is the concern,\" never \"here is what to do about it.\"\n- a `:coordinate-maintenance-order` proposal whose `:estimated-cost`\n  exceeds `maintenance-cost-escalation-threshold` (2000, inclusive\n  boundary verified by `.../ok-at-exact-cost-threshold-boundary`) --\n  always needs human sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an\n  ESCALATION, not a hard block: an over-budget maintenance order is not\n  itself unsafe, unlike an equipment-operation-finalizing or\n  safety-override attempt, which is why it is handled by a different\n  mechanism (soft escalate vs. structural absence + hard content\n  block).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op -- `.../escalates-low-confidence`.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, explicitly called out in this task's guidance)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"equipment\", \"movement\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal\n(the mock advisor's own template necessarily mentions equipment by\nname, e.g. \"...on equipment E-1\"), self-blocking the actor's own happy\npath. `farmforestryops.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases` avoids this by\nconstruction -- every entry is a multi-word finalization/execution\naction phrase (\"finalize the equipment movement\", \"initiate the\nequipment movement\", \"directly operate the equipment\", \"override the\noperator's on-site safety judgment\", etc.), never a bare noun. A\ndedicated regression test,\n`farmforestryops.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-\nself-trip-scope-violation`, drives every op in the closed allowlist\nthrough the default mock advisor and asserts none of them ever trips\n`:scope-violation`. Green on first run this session -- no self-trip was\nactually hit during independent re-verification, but this ADR still\ndocuments the guard and its regression test per the task's explicit\nrequirement.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8341\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to\n  the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8341` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8341`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before the edit, not\n  assumed, to account for concurrent sibling ISCO-track landings in\n  this same batch -- up to 8 sibling agents may be landing\n  concurrently).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 19 tests, 44 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/counts`\n  and in the task report, independently re-verified against a\n  brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just the\n  scaffold session's own report).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- consistent with the live convention already confirmed\n  by sibling ISCO actors (`cloud-itonami-isco-3313` does NOT depend on\n  `kotoba-lang/technology`; only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation`\n  registry itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies`\n  stacks).\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to tx-data\")\n  -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string blob rather\n  than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-equipment-movement` that is\n  merely gated behind `always-escalate-ops` (the shape a naive reading\n  of \"gate high-stakes actions behind human approval\" might suggest):\n  rejected outright per the explicit task guidance and the\n  ADR-2607152500 precedent (decisions directly touching a person's\n  safety must always exclude the closed op allowlist and always\n  escalate, not merely be gated behind confidence). For heavy mobile\n  equipment where a wrong movement decision risks rollover or\n  entanglement within seconds, the *capability* to influence the\n  movement decision must not exist in this actor's code path at all --\n  even an \"always-escalate\" op would create a UI/API surface that could\n  be pressured into a rubber-stamp under time pressure, or quietly\n  flipped to auto-commit by a future maintainer. The only op related to\n  safety escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need\n  for human attention and never itself decides, authorizes, or defers\n  an equipment-movement action for later automatic execution.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"equipment\"\n  \"movement\"]`): rejected outright per the task's explicit warning\n  about this fleet-known self-trip bug class -- this actor's own domain\n  necessarily discusses equipment by name/id to do its logging and\n  scheduling job (e.g. \"on equipment E-1\"), so a bare-noun list would\n  self-block the actor's own legitimate proposals. The actual\n  implementation uses full finalization/execution action phrases\n  exclusively, with a dedicated regression test proving the default\n  mock advisor never self-trips.\n- Allowing `:log-service-record` to carry a free-form \"action taken\"\n  field describing what the operator did with the equipment during the\n  logged session: considered, but rejected for this initial\n  implementation as a plausible seed for scope creep into recording (or\n  implicitly endorsing) an equipment-operation decision over time. The\n  op is scoped to usage/incident-report metadata only (equipment id,\n  hours, condition, incident description) with no field shaped like an\n  operation/movement authorization.\n- A numeric confidence-only gate with no content-based scope-exclusion\n  check: rejected as insufficient -- confidence measures the advisor's\n  certainty about the proposal it chose to make, not whether that\n  proposal's free text smuggles equipment-movement-finalization or\n  safety-override language; this is exactly why the scope-exclusion\n  check exists as an independent, content-based, permanent HARD block\n  regardless of confidence.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above.\n\n1. **`:log-service-record`** -- equipment usage/incident-report data\n   logging only (equipment id, usage hours, condition, incident\n   metadata). Cannot carry an equipment-operation/movement finalization\n   or a safety-judgment override -- any such content in `:rationale` is\n   HARD-blocked by the scope-exclusion check. It is a records-management\n   action, indistinguishable in kind from a mechanic's usage log.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- operator-roster and field-work\n   scheduling proposal only (which operator, which field/site, which\n   window). Never a live movement authorization for an active piece of\n   equipment -- it is a calendar action, gated by the same\n   operator/equipment provenance checks as every other op.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces an equipment-defect,\n   terrain-hazard, or operator-fatigue concern to a human; ALWAYS\n   escalates (governor's `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally, at\n   every confidence level), NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the\n   ONLY channel through which a safety observation may be surfaced, and\n   it explicitly never resolves, decides, or directs anything itself --\n   the human reviews and acts entirely on their own authority. It can\n   never be used to finalize an equipment-movement decision or override\n   an operator's on-site safety judgment -- the scope-exclusion check\n   additionally rejects any such content smuggled into its `:rationale`\n   field.\n4. **`:coordinate-maintenance-order`** -- equipment maintenance\n   procurement proposal (item, cost, vendor-equivalent). Not an\n   equipment-operation or movement action of any kind -- it is a\n   purchasing-logistics action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 2000 always escalates) like any equipment\n   procurement workflow.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na decision to finalize an equipment-operation/movement decision, or an\noverride of an operator's on-site safety judgment -- these are\nstructurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op\nallowlist itself (`farmforestryops.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) the\n`:op-not-allowlisted` HARD check rejecting any out-of-allowlist op\nunconditionally, (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent\nof the `:op` field, phrased exclusively as finalization/execution\nactions to avoid the fleet-known self-trip bug class\n(`farmforestryops.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases`, with a dedicated\nregression test\n`farmforestryops.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-violation`),\nand (d) an end-to-end test driving a rogue advisor (whose proposal\nalways attempts to finalize an equipment-operation/movement decision)\nthrough the full `langgraph.graph` actor graph, proving the StateGraph\nitself always resolves to `:hold` with zero records committed even when\nthe advisor node itself is compromised\n(`farmforestryops.actor-test/holds-on-scope-violation-and-is-never-overridable`).\n")))
